@prefix hsh:  <https://semantic.ds-labs.org/shapes/honeycomb#> .
@prefix hive: <https://semantic.ds-labs.org/vocab/honeycomb#> .
@prefix sh:   <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .
@prefix rdf:  <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix dct:  <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

#################################################################
# The SHACL contract for the honeycomb diagram vocabulary.
#
# WHAT IS ENFORCED WHERE, STATED SO A READER DOES NOT ASSUME MORE. The owning
# crate asserts these invariants in Rust, where most of them are unrepresentable
# rather than merely checked. This file restates the same contract for graph
# consumers - a hand-edited file, a file written by another tool, a file that
# arrived through a pull request. The only place it runs against a real corpus is
# a consumer's own validation gate; upstream CI runs it over a handful of
# committed fixtures and asserts this file's own meta-properties.
#
# FOUR CONVENTIONS, EACH OF WHICH IS A BUG SOMEBODY ALREADY HIT.
#
# 1. ONE NODE-LEVEL CONSTRAINT PER NODE SHAPE. A node shape's sh:message is
#    copied onto every result its NODE-LEVEL constraints produce - sh:closed,
#    sh:xone, sh:sparql. A shape carrying two of them prints one message for two
#    different failures, and one of the two is then wrong. hsh:PlacementShape
#    therefore owns sh:closed and nothing else, and the mutual exclusion lives in
#    its own shape, hsh:PlacementDrawsOneThing.
#
# 2. EVERY NODE THAT STATES A CONSTRAINT CARRIES AN sh:message, INCLUDING FOUR
#    THAT CAN NEVER BE SHOWN. The four nested property shapes inside
#    hsh:PlacementDrawsOneThing's sh:xone list are unreachable: pyshacl reports a
#    single result at the enclosing sh:xone and the inner messages are never
#    evaluated. They are written anyway because a consumer gate walks the shapes
#    graph for nodes carrying a core constraint parameter, and exempts only the
#    xone MEMBERS themselves - a nested property shape is one hop further out and
#    carries sh:minCount / sh:maxCount. Omitting them is four violations in the
#    gate, in the worst possible place: inside bytes a consumer has vendored and
#    cannot edit. The irony is worth writing down rather than hiding: a rule whose
#    purpose is that every message shown is readable requires four messages that
#    are not.
#
# 3. sh:severity GOES ON THE SHAPE, NEVER INSIDE AN sh:SPARQLConstraint, where it
#    is silently ignored and the rule reports as a Violation. hsh:GroupsDoNotTouch
#    is the only Warning here and it must stay one.
#
# 4. MESSAGES ARE rule: / why: / fix:. rule: names the invariant, why: names the
#    failure it prevents, fix: says what to type. A contributor who has never read
#    SHACL must be able to act on the message alone.
#
# A COUPLING WORTH KNOWING BEFORE YOU EDIT. sh:closed counts only the sh:path
# values of the property shapes of THE SAME node shape. The hive:represents and
# hive:tile paths inside hsh:PlacementDrawsOneThing's sh:xone contribute nothing
# to hsh:PlacementShape's closedness - it admits them only because
# hsh:PlacementShape declares both paths itself. Adding a path to the xone side
# alone makes hsh:PlacementShape reject it with the anti-drift message, which
# would read as nonsense. Add paths to both, or to neither.
#
# NO CONTIGUITY SHAPE, DELIBERATELY. Transitive closure over a lattice is not
# something SPARQL should be asked to do, and a fractured group is a legitimate
# drawing: an editor that permits regrouping must permit the transiently split
# state between pulling a member out and putting it back. It is computed in the
# component and reported in the interface.
#
# WHERE A LANGUAGE TAG IS ALLOWED, WHICH THIS FILE ALREADY DECIDES WITHOUT EVER
# USING THE WORD. A language-tagged literal has datatype rdf:langString, so a
# property shape carrying sh:datatype xsd:string REJECTS one and a property
# shape carrying no sh:datatype at all ACCEPTS one. That is not a subtlety this
# file may leave implicit any more: rdfs:label is constrained here with
# sh:minLength and deliberately no datatype on all four of hsh:DiagramShape,
# hsh:TileShape, hsh:GroupShape and hsh:LinkShape, and rdfs:comment carries no
# property shape at all - so "Plan de la mairie"@fr conforms, and the owning
# crate now carries the tag through its model and writes it back. Every other
# string-valued property here - hive:slug, hive:note, hive:generator,
# hive:pinnedRevision, hive:formatVersion, and hive:generatedAt with
# xsd:dateTime - pins a datatype, so a tag on one of those is a document THIS
# FILE rejects, and the crate refuses it by name rather than silently dropping
# the tag and writing back something the author did not write.
#
# SO ADDING OR REMOVING AN sh:datatype ON A STRING PROPERTY IS A DECISION ABOUT
# LANGUAGES, whether or not whoever makes it is thinking about languages.
# hive:note is the live example: it is prose printed under a heading and a
# perfectly reasonable thing to want in French, and it cannot carry a tag today
# because BOTH this file and ns.ttl's own rdfs:range say xsd:string. Relaxing
# that is a vocabulary change - a new owl:versionInfo here, a new rdfs:range in
# ns.ttl and ns, and hive:tboxVersion with them - and not something a reader or
# a writer may decide on its own.
#
# THERE IS NO @base HERE, for the same reason there is none in the vocabulary: a
# consumer that vendors a copy prepends its own, and that stays a prepend only
# because this file declares none and contains zero relative IRI refs.
#################################################################

<https://semantic.ds-labs.org/shapes/honeycomb> a owl:Ontology ;
  dct:title "SHACL shapes for the honeycomb diagram vocabulary"@en ;
  rdfs:seeAlso <https://github.com/ds-labs-org/ds-honeycomb-editor-rs> ;
  owl:versionInfo "0.3.0" ;
  # The prefix map every sh:SPARQLConstraint below resolves its names against.
  # Written out rather than assumed: a SPARQL constraint whose prefix is not
  # declared here does not fail loudly, it fails at parse time inside the
  # validator with a message that names no shape.
  sh:declare
    [ sh:prefix "hive" ; sh:namespace "https://semantic.ds-labs.org/vocab/honeycomb#"^^xsd:anyURI ] ,
    [ sh:prefix "rdf"  ; sh:namespace "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#"^^xsd:anyURI ] ,
    [ sh:prefix "rdfs" ; sh:namespace "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#"^^xsd:anyURI ] ,
    [ sh:prefix "xsd"  ; sh:namespace "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#"^^xsd:anyURI ] .

#############################  hsh:DiagramShape

# NOT closed. A host hangs its own predicates on a diagram, and typo-catching on
# an open class is the host's business: it declares its own closed shape locally.
hsh:DiagramShape a sh:NodeShape ;
  sh:targetClass hive:Diagram ;

  sh:property [
    sh:path hive:slug ; sh:datatype xsd:string ;
    sh:minCount 1 ; sh:maxCount 1 ;
    sh:pattern "^[a-z0-9]+(-[a-z0-9]+)*$" ;
    sh:message """rule: A hive:Diagram carries exactly one hive:slug, kebab-case.
why:  The slug is the last segment of the diagram IRI and the name a writer
      addresses the diagram by when it re-exports. Missing, doubled, capitalised
      or spaced, it stops matching, and the next export writes a second diagram
      beside this one instead of replacing it.
fix:  Give the diagram one hive:slug matching ^[a-z0-9]+(-[a-z0-9]+)*$ and equal
      to the last segment of its own IRI.""" ;
  ] ;

  sh:property [
    sh:path rdfs:label ; sh:minCount 1 ; sh:maxCount 1 ; sh:minLength 1 ;
    sh:message """rule: A hive:Diagram carries exactly one non-empty rdfs:label.
why:  The label is the only human name the diagram has. A reviewer meeting an
      untitled diagram in a diff has nothing to tell it from the next one, and
      the editor has nothing to put in its heading.
fix:  Add one rdfs:label carrying the title a reader should see.""" ;
  ] ;

  sh:property [
    sh:path hive:mode ; sh:minCount 1 ; sh:maxCount 1 ;
    sh:in ( hive:pinned hive:standalone ) ;
    sh:message """rule: A hive:Diagram carries exactly one hive:mode, hive:pinned or hive:standalone.
why:  The mode decides which properties a placement may carry, so it is stated
      rather than inferred: a diagram that had lost its last placement would
      otherwise change mode in silence, and an editor loading it could not know
      whether typing a label is allowed. A misspelled mode IRI is not a third
      mode, it is a file no reader can classify.
fix:  Set hive:mode to hive:pinned or hive:standalone. This is checked with sh:in
      rather than a pattern precisely so a near-miss spelling is caught too.""" ;
  ] ;

  sh:property [
    sh:path hive:lattice ; sh:minCount 1 ; sh:maxCount 1 ;
    sh:in ( hive:oddRPointyTop ) ;
    sh:message """rule: A hive:Diagram carries exactly one hive:lattice, and hive:oddRPointyTop is the only convention defined today.
why:  Two tools disagreeing about the convention produce the same integers and a
      different picture, with nothing anywhere to say so. A reader that meets a
      convention it does not implement must refuse rather than approximate, which
      it can only do if the file names one.
fix:  Set hive:lattice to hive:oddRPointyTop, or define the second convention in
      the vocabulary before writing a document in it.""" ;
  ] ;

  sh:property [
    sh:path hive:formatVersion ; sh:maxCount 1 ; sh:datatype xsd:string ;
    sh:pattern "^[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+$" ;
    sh:message """rule: A hive:Diagram carries at most one hive:formatVersion, MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH.
why:  This is the only marker in a saved file that says which vocabulary
      release its terms were written against. hive:generator is free text, is
      never required to name a version at all, and is overwritten by every
      writer that re-saves the file - none of which a reader can safely parse
      or trust. Without a stated version, an older reader has no way to know
      it is looking at a file that may use a term it has never heard of, and
      the loss is silent: it simply drops whatever it does not recognise.
fix:  State the MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH of the vocabulary release the writer
      targeted, for example \"0.3.0\". Optional today; a future major version
      of this vocabulary may require it.""" ;
  ] ;

  sh:property [
    sh:path hive:pinnedTo ; sh:maxCount 1 ; sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
    sh:message """rule: A hive:Diagram carries at most one hive:pinnedTo, and it is an IRI.
why:  Two sources is a layout of neither. A literal here names a source nothing
      can resolve, and this vocabulary deliberately never dereferences the value,
      so nothing downstream would notice.
fix:  Name the single external source this layout lays out as one IRI. Whether
      that IRI resolves is a question only the consuming repository can answer,
      and it answers it in a shape of its own.""" ;
  ] ;

  sh:property [
    sh:path hive:pinnedRevision ; sh:maxCount 1 ; sh:datatype xsd:string ;
    sh:message """rule: A hive:Diagram carries at most one hive:pinnedRevision, a string.
why:  It is the drift detector: compared against the source current revision it
      is what lets a reader be told that this layout predates the data, instead
      of being shown a diagram that is quietly one cell short. Two revisions
      detect nothing.
fix:  Record one revision of the pinned source. The spelling - digest, git sha,
      version string - belongs to the host, which pins it in its own shape.""" ;
  ] ;

  sh:property [
    sh:path hive:placement ; sh:minCount 1 ; sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
    sh:message """rule: A hive:Diagram carries at least one hive:placement, and every value is an IRI.
why:  A diagram with no placement draws nothing: that is a saved file which lost
      its contents, not an empty canvas worth keeping. A placement written as a
      blank node is invisible to any gate that selects subjects by IRI prefix, so
      it would be the single unchecked thing in an otherwise checked file.
fix:  Give the diagram its placements as IRIs under the document own subject
      namespace.""" ;
  ] ;

  sh:property [
    sh:path hive:group ; sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
    sh:message """rule: Every hive:group value of a hive:Diagram is an IRI.
why:  A group is named by placements and by the diagram that declares it; a blank
      node cannot be named from a second place, so a blank-node group can never be
      the one a placement points at.
fix:  Declare each group as an IRI subject. Groups are optional - a diagram with
      none is fine.""" ;
  ] ;

  sh:property [
    sh:path hive:note ; sh:maxCount 1 ; sh:datatype xsd:string ;
    sh:message """rule: A hive:Diagram carries at most one hive:note, a string.
why:  The note is one line printed under the heading. Two of them is two lines
      with no order between them, and the renderer would have to invent one.
fix:  Merge the notes into one, or move the detail to rdfs:comment, which says
      what the diagram IS rather than what a reader should know while looking.""" ;
  ] ;

  sh:property [
    sh:path hive:generator ; sh:maxCount 1 ; sh:datatype xsd:string ;
    sh:message """rule: A hive:Diagram carries at most one hive:generator, a string naming the writer and its version.
why:  An exported diagram arrives through a clipboard or a download rather than
      through a script anybody can re-run, so the file itself is the only place
      its provenance can live. Two generators is two claims about who wrote one
      file.
fix:  Record the writing tool once, as name and version.""" ;
  ] ;

  sh:property [
    sh:path hive:generatedAt ; sh:maxCount 1 ; sh:datatype xsd:dateTime ;
    sh:pattern "(Z|[+-][0-9]{2}:[0-9]{2})$" ;
    sh:message """rule: A hive:Diagram carries at most one hive:generatedAt, an xsd:dateTime with an explicit time zone.
why:  Read against the pinned source own last change, this timestamp says whether
      the layout was placed before or after the thing it lays out last moved. A
      dateTime with no zone compares wrongly the moment a CI runner sits in a
      different one, and xsd:dateTime alone does not require the zone - the
      pattern does.
fix:  Write the timestamp with a zone, for example 2026-01-31T09:12:44Z.""" ;
  ] .

#############################  hsh:PlacementShape - THE ONE CLOSED SHAPE

#################################################################
# THE CLOSED SHAPE IS OPT-IN UNLESS SOMETHING DEMANDS THE TYPE, and that is a
# hole in the vocabulary's central promise rather than a nicety.
#
# hsh:PlacementShape targets `a hive:Placement`. A writer who simply OMITS that
# type escapes it entirely — and with it sh:closed, the one mechanism that makes
# "a pinned layout cannot cache a label" true. Reproduced with pyshacl: a pinned
# diagram whose placement carried hive:col, hive:row, hive:represents AND an
# rdfs:label, with no rdf:type, validated clean.
#
# So the type is required of anything that behaves like a placement. Targeting
# hive:col rather than hive:inGroup or hive:represents because col is the one
# property every placement has in both modes and nothing else in the vocabulary
# uses: a subject carrying a lattice column IS a placement, whatever it calls
# itself.
#################################################################

hsh:PlacementsAreTyped a sh:NodeShape ;
  sh:targetSubjectsOf hive:col ;
  sh:message """rule: anything carrying hive:col must be declared `a hive:Placement`.
why:  the closed-shape guarantee — that a placement cannot carry content — is
      enforced by a shape that targets the CLASS. An untyped subject with a
      column and a row is a placement in every way that matters to a renderer and
      in none that matter to the validator, so it may carry a cached label the
      vocabulary promises is impossible.
fix:  add `a hive:Placement` to the subject.""" ;
  sh:class hive:Placement .

hsh:PlacementShape a sh:NodeShape ;
  sh:targetClass hive:Placement ;
  sh:closed true ;
  sh:ignoredProperties ( rdf:type ) ;
  sh:message """rule: A hive:Placement carries nothing beyond hive:col, hive:row, hive:inGroup and one of hive:represents or hive:tile.
why:  THIS IS THE ANTI-DRIFT RULE. A label, a comment, a symbol or a layer copied
      onto a placement is content cached beside a layout, and nothing anywhere
      invalidates it: the source changes, the copy does not, and the diagram
      quietly shows last year. This is the one closed shape in the contract, so
      caching is not a rule a contributor has to remember - it is a file that will
      not validate.
fix:  Delete the extra predicate. In hive:pinned mode the content belongs to the
      subject named by hive:represents; in hive:standalone mode it belongs on the
      hive:Tile named by hive:tile, which is left open for exactly this.""" ;

  sh:property [
    sh:path hive:col ; sh:datatype xsd:integer ; sh:minCount 1 ; sh:maxCount 1 ;
    sh:message """rule: A hive:Placement carries exactly one hive:col, an xsd:integer which may be negative.
why:  With hive:row it IS the cell. A placement missing a coordinate is a tile
      with no position, which a renderer can only drop or pile at the origin. The
      origin is wherever the first tile landed rather than a corner, so negative
      columns are ordinary and an implementation that indexes a grid from zero
      loses them without saying so.
fix:  Give the placement one hive:col as a plain integer, negative if that is
      where the cell is.""" ;
  ] ;

  sh:property [
    sh:path hive:row ; sh:datatype xsd:integer ; sh:minCount 1 ; sh:maxCount 1 ;
    sh:message """rule: A hive:Placement carries exactly one hive:row, an xsd:integer which may be negative.
why:  With hive:col it IS the cell, and under hive:oddRPointyTop the row parity
      decides which way the row is shifted, so a missing or duplicated row is not
      a missing number but a cell in the wrong place. Whether two placements share
      a cell is checked separately, by hsh:NoTwoInOneCell.
fix:  Give the placement one hive:row as a plain integer.""" ;
  ] ;

  sh:property [
    sh:path hive:inGroup ; sh:maxCount 1 ; sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
    sh:message """rule: A hive:Placement names at most one hive:inGroup, and it is an IRI.
why:  Membership is a fact about the diagram, not about an editor's gestures. An
      editor that offers to move a whole group translates every placement sharing
      this value rigidly; one that offers to move a single placement leaves the
      rest where they are and reports the group as being in more than one piece.
      Either way the value survives the move. Two groups would make one placement
      follow two regions and arrive at neither.
fix:  Name one group, or none. That the group is one this diagram declares is
      checked by hsh:GroupBelongsToItsDiagram.""" ;
  ] ;

  sh:property [
    sh:path hive:represents ; sh:maxCount 1 ; sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
    sh:message """rule: A hive:Placement names at most one hive:represents, and it is an IRI.
why:  Two external subjects in one cell is two drawings for one hexagon with no
      precedence between them, so which wins depends on statement order. A literal
      here is content cached on a placement under a different name, which is
      exactly the drift the closed shape exists to stop.
fix:  Name one external subject as an IRI in the host own namespace. Whether that
      subject still exists is a question only the consuming repository can answer,
      and it answers it in a shape of its own.""" ;
  ] ;

  sh:property [
    sh:path hive:tile ; sh:maxCount 1 ; sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
    # sh:class, not merely sh:nodeKind: "standalone means self-contained" is the
    # whole trade that mode makes, and without this a placement may name a tile
    # that is not in the document at all. Reproduced: hive:tile pointing at
    # <https://elsewhere.example.net/tiles#ghost>, with no hive:Tile anywhere,
    # validated clean. sh:class requires the object to BE a hive:Tile in the
    # graph being validated, which is exactly the containment claim.
    sh:class hive:Tile ;
    sh:message """rule: A hive:Placement names at most one hive:tile, and it is an IRI.
why:  Two tiles in one cell is two contents for one hexagon with no precedence.
      A blank-node tile cannot be named by anything else in the document, so
      nothing could ever point a second placement at the same content.
fix:  Name one hive:Tile declared in this same document.""" ;
  ] .

#############################  hsh:PlacementDrawsOneThing

# Its own node shape because sh:xone is node-level and hsh:PlacementShape has
# already spent its one node-level constraint on sh:closed (convention 1 above).
hsh:PlacementDrawsOneThing a sh:NodeShape ;
  sh:targetClass hive:Placement ;
  sh:message """rule: A hive:Placement carries hive:represents OR hive:tile, never both and never neither.
why:  A cell that draws nothing is an empty hexagon which still reserves a lattice
      position, so the next drop onto it is refused for a reason nothing on screen
      explains. A cell that draws twice is two contents with no precedence, and
      which one wins depends on statement order - so the same file draws
      differently after an unrelated re-export.
fix:  In a hive:pinned diagram give the placement one hive:represents; in a
      hive:standalone diagram give it one hive:tile.""" ;
  sh:xone (
    [
      sh:property [
        sh:path hive:represents ; sh:minCount 1 ;
        sh:message """rule: The pinned branch of hsh:PlacementDrawsOneThing requires hive:represents.
why:  UNREACHABLE, AND WRITTEN ANYWAY - see convention 2 at the head of this file.
      A single result is reported at the enclosing sh:xone and this message is
      never evaluated; a consumer gate nevertheless requires an sh:message on
      every node stating a constraint, and sh:minCount is one.
fix:  Read the hsh:PlacementDrawsOneThing message instead - it is the one a
      contributor is shown.""" ;
      ] ;
      sh:property [
        sh:path hive:tile ; sh:maxCount 0 ;
        sh:message """rule: The pinned branch of hsh:PlacementDrawsOneThing forbids hive:tile.
why:  UNREACHABLE, AND WRITTEN ANYWAY - see convention 2 at the head of this file.
      A single result is reported at the enclosing sh:xone and this message is
      never evaluated; a consumer gate nevertheless requires an sh:message on
      every node stating a constraint, and sh:maxCount is one.
fix:  Read the hsh:PlacementDrawsOneThing message instead - it is the one a
      contributor is shown.""" ;
      ] ;
    ]
    [
      sh:property [
        sh:path hive:tile ; sh:minCount 1 ;
        sh:message """rule: The standalone branch of hsh:PlacementDrawsOneThing requires hive:tile.
why:  UNREACHABLE, AND WRITTEN ANYWAY - see convention 2 at the head of this file.
      A single result is reported at the enclosing sh:xone and this message is
      never evaluated; a consumer gate nevertheless requires an sh:message on
      every node stating a constraint, and sh:minCount is one.
fix:  Read the hsh:PlacementDrawsOneThing message instead - it is the one a
      contributor is shown.""" ;
      ] ;
      sh:property [
        sh:path hive:represents ; sh:maxCount 0 ;
        sh:message """rule: The standalone branch of hsh:PlacementDrawsOneThing forbids hive:represents.
why:  UNREACHABLE, AND WRITTEN ANYWAY - see convention 2 at the head of this file.
      A single result is reported at the enclosing sh:xone and this message is
      never evaluated; a consumer gate nevertheless requires an sh:message on
      every node stating a constraint, and sh:maxCount is one.
fix:  Read the hsh:PlacementDrawsOneThing message instead - it is the one a
      contributor is shown.""" ;
      ] ;
    ]
  ) .

#############################  hsh:TileShape

# NOT closed, and that is the point: a host owns tile content, adds its own
# predicates here, and an editor round-tripping a standalone diagram must carry
# back predicates it does not understand.
hsh:TileShape a sh:NodeShape ;
  sh:targetClass hive:Tile ;

  sh:property [
    sh:path hive:slug ; sh:datatype xsd:string ;
    sh:minCount 1 ; sh:maxCount 1 ;
    sh:pattern "^[a-z0-9]+(-[a-z0-9]+)*$" ;
    sh:message """rule: A hive:Tile carries exactly one hive:slug, kebab-case.
why:  The slug is the last segment of the tile own IRI and the name a writer
      addresses it by on re-export. When it stops matching, the re-export writes a
      second tile beside the first and the placement points at whichever the
      reader happens to choose.
fix:  Give the tile one hive:slug matching ^[a-z0-9]+(-[a-z0-9]+)*$ and equal to
      the last segment of its own IRI.""" ;
  ] ;

  sh:property [
    sh:path rdfs:label ; sh:minCount 1 ; sh:maxCount 1 ; sh:minLength 1 ;
    sh:message """rule: A hive:Tile carries exactly one non-empty rdfs:label.
why:  In a standalone diagram the tile is the only place the hexagon text can come
      from - nothing outside the document is read. A tile with no label draws an
      empty hexagon which still occupies its cell, so the next drop onto it is
      refused for a reason nothing on screen explains.
fix:  Add one rdfs:label with the text the hexagon should show.""" ;
  ] ;

  sh:property [
    sh:path hive:styleKey ; sh:maxCount 1 ; sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
    sh:message """rule: A hive:Tile carries at most one hive:styleKey, and it is an IRI.
why:  Two style keys is two appearances with no precedence between them. The key
      is opaque to this vocabulary - it is the seam that keeps a colour scheme out
      of a diagram format - so nothing here could ever arbitrate. An IRI rather
      than a string so keys cannot collide across hosts and so a host shape can
      check the key still resolves to a term that exists.
fix:  Name one style key as an IRI, or none and let the host draw its default.""" ;
  ] .

#############################  hsh:GroupShape

hsh:GroupShape a sh:NodeShape ;
  sh:targetClass hive:Group ;

  sh:property [
    sh:path hive:slug ; sh:datatype xsd:string ;
    sh:minCount 1 ; sh:maxCount 1 ;
    sh:pattern "^[a-z0-9]+(-[a-z0-9]+)*$" ;
    sh:message """rule: A hive:Group carries exactly one hive:slug, kebab-case.
why:  The slug is the last segment of the group own IRI and the name a writer
      addresses it by on re-export. When the two disagree, the re-export writes a
      second group beside the first and half the placements point at each.
fix:  Give the group one hive:slug matching ^[a-z0-9]+(-[a-z0-9]+)*$ and equal to
      the last segment of its own IRI.""" ;
  ] ;

  sh:property [
    sh:path rdfs:label ; sh:maxCount 1 ; sh:minLength 1 ;
    sh:message """rule: A hive:Group carries at most one rdfs:label, and if it carries one it is not empty.
why:  A group is drawn as a region behind its members, and a label is usually the
      thing saying why those cells are together - without one the reader gets a
      coloured blob that asserts a relationship and never names it. USUALLY, not
      always: where the ground itself is the whole signal, a heading says the same
      thing twice, and a diagram whose clusters are obvious should be allowed to
      stay quiet. So a group may have no label at all. What it may not have is an
      EMPTY one: rdfs:label "" claims the name is the empty string rather than
      that there is none, and a reader cannot tell the difference.
fix:  Give it one rdfs:label naming what the members have in common, or omit the
      predicate entirely. Do not write an empty string.""" ;
  ] ;

  sh:property [
    sh:path hive:styleKey ; sh:maxCount 1 ; sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
    sh:message """rule: A hive:Group carries at most one hive:styleKey, and it is an IRI.
why:  Two style keys is two appearances for one region with no precedence. The
      region ground is often the only mark separating one kind of group from
      another, so the wrong one asserts a claim about the diagram rather than just
      looking wrong.
fix:  Name one style key as an IRI, or none and let the host draw its default.""" ;
  ] ;

  sh:property [
    sh:path hive:note ; sh:maxCount 1 ; sh:datatype xsd:string ;
    sh:message """rule: A hive:Group carries at most one hive:note, a string.
why:  The note is one line printed under the group heading - a multiplier on a
      replicated stack, a caveat. Two of them is two lines with no order between
      them, and the renderer would have to invent one.
fix:  Merge the notes into one, or move the detail to rdfs:comment, which says
      what the group IS rather than what a reader should know while looking.""" ;
  ] .

#############################################################
# The rules that earn the vocabulary. Each sh:SPARQLConstraint carries its own
# sh:message and the owning node shape carries none, so that message survives.
#############################################################

hsh:NoTwoInOneCell a sh:NodeShape ;
  sh:targetClass hive:Diagram ;
  sh:sparql [
    a sh:SPARQLConstraint ;
    sh:prefixes <https://semantic.ds-labs.org/shapes/honeycomb> ;
    sh:message """rule: Two placements of one diagram may not share a cell.
why:  An overlap is invisible in Turtle and unmistakable on screen: one hexagon
      simply covers another, and which one wins depends on statement order, so the
      same file draws differently after an unrelated re-export. The editor makes
      this unrepresentable at rest - the cell is the key of the map it stores
      placements in - so a file reaching here with one was hand-edited or written
      by something else.
fix:  Move one of the two placements to a free cell, or delete the duplicate.
      sh:value names the earlier of the pair.""" ;
    sh:select """
      SELECT DISTINCT $this ?value WHERE {
        $this hive:placement ?value , ?other .
        ?value hive:col ?c ; hive:row ?r .
        ?other hive:col ?c ; hive:row ?r .
        FILTER ( STR(?value) < STR(?other) )
      }
    """ ;
  ] .

hsh:ModeIsHonest a sh:NodeShape ;
  sh:targetClass hive:Diagram ;

  sh:sparql [
    a sh:SPARQLConstraint ;
    sh:prefixes <https://semantic.ds-labs.org/shapes/honeycomb> ;
    sh:message """rule: A hive:pinned diagram has no placement carrying hive:tile.
why:  A pinned layout draws every cell from the source it is pinned to. A
      placement naming a local hive:Tile is a cell frozen at whatever that tile
      says while every cell beside it still tracks the source - a diagram half
      tracking its data and half showing last year, with nothing on the page
      distinguishing the halves.
fix:  Delete the hive:tile and give the placement a hive:represents, or set the
      diagram hive:mode to hive:standalone and convert every placement.""" ;
    sh:select """
      SELECT DISTINCT $this ?value WHERE {
        $this hive:mode hive:pinned ; hive:placement ?value .
        ?value hive:tile ?t .
      }
    """ ;
  ] ;

  sh:sparql [
    a sh:SPARQLConstraint ;
    sh:prefixes <https://semantic.ds-labs.org/shapes/honeycomb> ;
    sh:message """rule: A hive:standalone diagram carries no hive:pinnedTo, and none of its placements carries hive:represents.
why:  Standalone means nothing outside the document is read, which is the whole
      trade it makes: nothing regenerates it and nothing can make it stale. A
      hive:pinnedTo or a hive:represents claims otherwise, and the document then
      looks regenerable to a reader while no regeneration will ever touch it.
fix:  Delete the offending value, or set hive:mode to hive:pinned and give every
      placement a hive:represents. sh:value names what pointed outside.""" ;
    sh:select """
      SELECT DISTINCT $this ?value WHERE {
        $this hive:mode hive:standalone .
        { $this hive:pinnedTo ?value }
        UNION
        { $this hive:placement ?value . ?value hive:represents ?subject }
      }
    """ ;
  ] ;

  sh:sparql [
    a sh:SPARQLConstraint ;
    sh:prefixes <https://semantic.ds-labs.org/shapes/honeycomb> ;
    sh:message """rule: A hive:pinned diagram names the source it is pinned to, with hive:pinnedTo.
why:  A layout of an unnamed source is a layout of nothing in particular. Loaded
      against the wrong source it silently drops every placement whose subject is
      absent there and still looks like a diagram, one cell short and saying
      nothing about which cell it dropped.
fix:  Add one hive:pinnedTo naming the external source as an IRI.""" ;
    sh:select """
      SELECT DISTINCT $this WHERE {
        $this hive:mode hive:pinned .
        FILTER NOT EXISTS { $this hive:pinnedTo ?source }
      }
    """ ;
  ] .

hsh:GroupBelongsToItsDiagram a sh:NodeShape ;
  sh:targetClass hive:Diagram ;
  sh:sparql [
    a sh:SPARQLConstraint ;
    sh:prefixes <https://semantic.ds-labs.org/shapes/honeycomb> ;
    sh:message """rule: Every group a placement names is declared by the same diagram with hive:group.
why:  A group borrowed from another document makes a group move here reach cells
      there, because membership is what a group move acts on. A group declared in neither
      document has no label, no style key and no members any reader can
      enumerate - it draws as a region with nothing to say about itself.
fix:  Add hive:group to this diagram naming the group, or point the placement at a
      group this diagram already declares. sh:value names the group.""" ;
    sh:select """
      SELECT DISTINCT $this ?value WHERE {
        $this hive:placement ?placement .
        ?placement hive:inGroup ?value .
        FILTER NOT EXISTS { $this hive:group ?value }
      }
    """ ;
  ] .

hsh:PlacementBelongsToOneDiagram a sh:NodeShape ;
  sh:targetClass hive:Placement ;
  sh:sparql [
    a sh:SPARQLConstraint ;
    sh:prefixes <https://semantic.ds-labs.org/shapes/honeycomb> ;
    sh:message """rule: A hive:Placement belongs to exactly one hive:Diagram.
why:  A placement named by two diagrams is one cell in two drawings: dragging it
      in one moves it in the other, and from that moment the two disagree about
      their own contents. hsh:GroupBelongsToItsDiagram cannot see this - it looks
      outwards from one diagram and each diagram alone is consistent.
fix:  Give each diagram its own placement subjects; they are cheap and carry
      nothing worth sharing. sh:value names the second diagram.""" ;
    sh:select """
      SELECT DISTINCT $this ?value WHERE {
        ?first hive:placement $this .
        ?value hive:placement $this .
        FILTER ( STR(?first) < STR(?value) )
      }
    """ ;
  ] .

hsh:SlugMatchesIri a sh:NodeShape ;
  sh:targetSubjectsOf hive:slug ;
  sh:sparql [
    a sh:SPARQLConstraint ;
    sh:prefixes <https://semantic.ds-labs.org/shapes/honeycomb> ;
    sh:message """rule: A hive:slug equals the last segment of its own subject IRI.
why:  A writer addresses subjects by slug when it re-exports. When the two
      disagree the re-export writes a second subject beside the first rather than
      replacing it, and the document quietly gains a duplicate that every later
      read has to choose between.
fix:  Rename the slug to the last segment of the IRI, or move the subject to the
      IRI its slug implies. sh:value names the slug that disagrees.""" ;
    sh:select """
      SELECT DISTINCT $this ?value WHERE {
        $this hive:slug ?value .
        FILTER ( REPLACE( STR($this), "^.*[/#]", "" ) != STR(?value) )
      }
    """ ;
  ] .

# WARNING, NOT VIOLATION, and the severity is on the SHAPE because inside an
# sh:SPARQLConstraint it is silently ignored and the rule reports as a Violation.
# A real published diagram already contains one such pair - measured, not assumed -
# so a rule that rejected it would reject the very drawing this vocabulary was
# written to carry.
hsh:GroupsDoNotTouch a sh:NodeShape ;
  sh:targetClass hive:Diagram ;
  sh:severity sh:Warning ;
  sh:sparql [
    a sh:SPARQLConstraint ;
    sh:prefixes <https://semantic.ds-labs.org/shapes/honeycomb> ;
    sh:message """rule: Cells of two different groups are edge-adjacent, so the two regions will merge when drawn.
why:  A group region is drawn by growing every member hexagon until adjacent ones
      overlap, which is what makes the region follow its members for free. Two
      groups whose cells touch therefore produce one continuous blob across two
      grounds, and the ground is often the only mark separating one kind of group
      from another. This is a warning on purpose: the arrangement is legal, and
      refusing it would refuse diagrams that already exist.
fix:  Move one group a cell away if the merge would mislead, or leave it and know
      the two regions will read as one. sh:value names the first group of the
      pair.""" ;
    sh:select """
      SELECT DISTINCT $this ?value WHERE {
        $this hive:placement ?pa , ?pb .
        ?pa hive:inGroup ?ga ; hive:col ?ca ; hive:row ?ra .
        ?pb hive:inGroup ?gb ; hive:col ?cb ; hive:row ?rb .
        FILTER ( STR(?ga) < STR(?gb) )
        BIND ( ?ra - ( 2 * FLOOR( ?ra / 2 ) ) AS ?s )
        FILTER ( ( ?rb = ?ra && ABS(?cb - ?ca) = 1 ) ||
                 ( ABS(?rb - ?ra) = 1 && ( ?cb = ?ca - 1 + ?s || ?cb = ?ca + ?s ) ) )
        BIND ( ?ga AS ?value )
      }
    """ ;
  ] .

hsh:LinkShape a sh:NodeShape ;
  sh:targetClass hive:Link ;
  rdfs:label "Link"@en ;
  # NOT CLOSED, for hive:Tile's reason: a host annotates its own lines - a
  # protocol, a cardinality, a colour of its own - and an editor that round-trips
  # a document must give back the predicates it did not understand.
  sh:property [
    sh:path hive:from ; sh:minCount 1 ; sh:maxCount 1 ; sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
    sh:class hive:Placement ;
    sh:message """rule: A hive:Link names exactly one hive:from, and it is a hive:Placement.
why:  A link with no start, or one whose start is not a placement, is a line the
      renderer has no cell to draw from. It draws nothing, which looks exactly
      like a link that was never there - so the file asserts a connection and the
      picture silently omits it.
fix:  Point hive:from at a hive:Placement this diagram declares with hive:placement.""" ;
  ] ;
  sh:property [
    sh:path hive:to ; sh:minCount 1 ; sh:maxCount 1 ; sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
    sh:class hive:Placement ;
    sh:message """rule: A hive:Link names exactly one hive:to, and it is a hive:Placement.
why:  As hive:from. A link is directed: undirected is a special case of directed
      and not the reverse, so both ends are required and which is which is kept.
fix:  Point hive:to at a hive:Placement this diagram declares with hive:placement.""" ;
  ] ;
  sh:property [
    sh:path hive:routing ; sh:maxCount 1 ; sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
    sh:in ( hive:straight hive:latticePath hive:arc ) ;
    sh:message """rule: A hive:Link carries at most one hive:routing, from the closed set of three.
why:  Routing is geometry rather than appearance: where a line goes is a question
      about the lattice, and two hosts drawing the same document must put it in
      the same place or they are drawing different diagrams. A fourth value would
      be a fourth renderer, and a reader meeting one cannot approximate it -
      there is no 'roughly straight'.
fix:  Use hive:straight, hive:latticePath or hive:arc, or omit it - absent means
      hive:straight.""" ;
  ] ;
  sh:property [
    sh:path rdfs:label ; sh:maxCount 1 ; sh:minLength 1 ;
    sh:message """rule: A hive:Link carries at most one rdfs:label, and if it carries one it is not empty.
why:  Most lines say enough by existing. One that needs words - 'registers offers',
      'once per participant' - gets one, and an empty string claims the label IS
      empty rather than that there is none.
fix:  Give it a label or omit the predicate.""" ;
  ] .

hsh:LinkEndsBelongToItsDiagram a sh:NodeShape ;
  sh:targetClass hive:Diagram ;
  rdfs:label "A link joins two placements of the same diagram"@en ;
  sh:sparql [
    a sh:SPARQLConstraint ;
    sh:prefixes <https://semantic.ds-labs.org/shapes/honeycomb> ;
    sh:message """rule: Both ends of every hive:link are placements this same diagram declares.
why:  hsh:LinkShape checks that an end IS a placement; it cannot check WHOSE. A
      link reaching into another diagram's placement draws a line from a cell
      this drawing does not contain, and a corpus holding two diagrams in one
      file is the ordinary case rather than an exotic one.
fix:  Point both ends at placements this diagram names with hive:placement.
      sh:value names the offending end.""" ;
    sh:select """
      SELECT DISTINCT $this ?value WHERE {
        $this hive:link ?link .
        ?link hive:from|hive:to ?value .
        FILTER NOT EXISTS { $this hive:placement ?value }
      }
    """ ;
  ] .

hsh:NoLinkToItself a sh:NodeShape ;
  sh:targetClass hive:Link ;
  rdfs:label "A link joins two different placements"@en ;
  sh:sparql [
    a sh:SPARQLConstraint ;
    sh:prefixes <https://semantic.ds-labs.org/shapes/honeycomb> ;
    sh:message """rule: hive:from and hive:to name different placements.
why:  A link from a cell to itself has no direction, no length and nothing to
      draw. It is not a diagram that says something unusual, it is a statement
      with no picture.
fix:  Point the two ends at different placements, or drop the link.""" ;
    sh:select """
      SELECT DISTINCT $this WHERE {
        $this hive:from ?a ; hive:to ?a .
      }
    """ ;
  ] .
